"""
The first board's last layer, with a converter of one more bit.

pta_board_range.py bought a range that clips nothing by fitting the last
layer's converter to its declared reference, and found what that costs: the
five networks of thirty whose range it widens have a fifth more error.  It
said the other way to buy range was not modelled: a converter with one more
bit at the same LSB.  This is that, and the other thing a ninth bit can be
spent on.

    nine bits          the step training's rule gives, which is the 8-bit
                       converter's, and 255 codes where it has 127: twice
                       the range at the same LSB
    nine bits, fitted  the fitted range, which is the 8-bit fitted
                       converter's, in steps half the size

WHAT EACH IS, IN LIGHT.  B14's noise is in LSB of an 8-bit converter: 0.25
of one at the receiver, 30 photons to one.  With nine bits at the same LSB a
sum has the light it had and the noise it had, and the receiver has to stay
linear to twice the photocurrent: one more bit of dynamic range, which is
what a converter of nine effective bits is.  Fitted with nine bits the top of
the range has the light it had with eight, so a network whose fitted range is
twice training's has half the light to a sum, as it has fitted with eight:
its noise is in the LSB of the 8-bit converter of that range, and only the
rounding is finer.  That is how grx930's harness has the two.

MEASURED IN A MODEL, by grx930 (c930/doc/pta_error_model_design_note.md section
5, "The first board's last layer, with a converter of one more bit",
2026-10-13; `SHAPE=chain sim/pta_mnist.sh DIR WORK chainbit`, and `pta_mnist
eval --gatebits B`).  pta_board_chain.py's networks, tile and draws, and its
format alone and its tile as budgeted, each four ways: with eight bits and
the range training's rule gives, which are that file's runs, line for line;
with eight bits, fitted, which are pta_board_range.py's; and the two with
nine.  On 10,000 images and on the first thousand.  A row with nine bits is
read over its own row with eight at the same range, draw for draw.

PRICED by this plan's own model: pta_power.py's converter, which is the best
and the fifth-best published part of at least that many effective bits at
the rate (pta_adc_survey.py), and pta_tighten.py's interface chip around it.

DERIVED here:

  a row's mean and  which have to be the seven tables grx930 printed, cell
  its error         for cell.  A row with eight bits is worked out from
                    pta_board_chain.py's and pta_board_range.py's figures,
                    which this file does not hold again
  what a row adds   how often right in the row it is read over, less in the
                    row, network by network
  what a way costs  a network's rms error one way over its rms error
                    another, draw for draw: the two are against one
                    reference, so it is a ratio of errors in the sum's own
                    units
  in steps, and of  as pta_board_chain.py has them, at the row's own step
  full scale        and its own count of codes

HOW SURE.  Five networks, 2.8 errors for one in twenty.  A run with nine bits
at training's step whose run with eight had no sum past its range is that
run, figure for figure, so what the bit adds there is the runs that were
clipped and nothing else.  Fitted with nine bits every run is another run.

WHAT THIS IS NOT.  grxgpu's classifier, or a chip: everything
pta_board_chain.py says of itself.  A range that holds by rule: with nine
bits at training's step nothing was clipped in these thirty networks, and
nothing says none ever is.  A receiver's price: the plan has one figure for
a receiver whatever its range, so one that is linear to twice the
photocurrent costs here what the other does.  A lit tile.

Standard library only.  Run:  python3 docs/designs/pta_board_bits.py
"""
import contextlib
import io
import math
import os
import sys

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
with contextlib.redirect_stdout(io.StringIO()):
    import pta_board_chain as chain
    import pta_board_range as fitted
    import pta_power as power
    import pta_tighten as tighten

MNIST, FASHION, INVERTED = chain.MNIST, chain.FASHION, chain.INVERTED
WORKLOADS, KINDS = chain.WORKLOADS, chain.KINDS
BEFORE, REFERENCE = chain.BEFORE, chain.REFERENCE
SEEDS, DRAWS, PLAN, T95 = chain.SEEDS, chain.DRAWS, chain.PLAN, chain.T95
ALL, FEW = chain.ALL, chain.FEW                # the test set, and its first thousand
OUTPUTS = chain.OUTPUTS
GATE_RMS, GATE_MAX, GATE_CLASS = chain.GATE_RMS, chain.GATE_MAX, chain.GATE_CLASS
HOST, DECLARED_CLASS = chain.HOST, chain.DECLARED_CLASS
TRAIN, FIT = fitted.TRAIN, fitted.FIT          # eight bits: the range training's rule gives, and fitted
NINE, NINEFIT = "nine", "ninefit"              # nine bits: at training's step, and fitted
BITS = {TRAIN: 8, FIT: 8, NINE: 9, NINEFIT: 9}
CODES = {8: chain.TOP, 9: 2 * chain.TOP + 1}   # a converter's full scale, in its own steps
UNDER = {NINE: TRAIN, NINEFIT: FIT}            # the row with eight bits a row with nine is read over
CORES = fitted.CORES
WAYS = ((TRAIN, "eight bits"), (FIT, "eight bits, fitted"), (NINE, "nine bits"), (NINEFIT, "nine bits, fitted"))
BASES = tuple((core, way, images) for images in (ALL, FEW) for way, _ in WAYS for core, _ in CORES)
NAME = dict(CORES)
WAY = dict(WAYS)
COLUMNS = tighten.TILE[1]                      # the tile's converters: one a column
assert len(BASES) == 16 and CODES == {8: 127, 9: 255} and DRAWS == (0, 1, 2, 3, 4) and COLUMNS == 64

